// Bin-parity — array-output XOR-combine accumulator (TASK-0343.01.01).
//
// Input is a stream of N i32 values, each constrained to the bin
// range [0, BINS). The output is a BINS-element i32 array where
// `parity[b]` is the PARITY (count mod 2) of input elements that
// equal `b`: 1 if an odd number of inputs equal `b`, else 0.
//
// This is the XOR sibling of 08-histogram (which SUMS per-bin hits).
// It exists to exercise the NEW non-`wrapping_add` combine arm of the
// overlapping-write accumulator fan-in (TASK-0343.01.01): the kernel
// declares `combine=xor`, so the host element-wise combine of the
// per-worker partials emits `parity[_k] = parity[_k] ^ _tmp[_k]`
// (operator form) instead of `.wrapping_add(...)` (method form).
//
// Why XOR is the right thing to demonstrate
// -----------------------------------------
//   - XOR is associative + commutative ⇒ the cross-worker combine is
//     ORDER-INDEPENDENT across worker arrival ⇒ bit-identical across
//     all 7 tier-1 backends (PRD §10.1). This is a STRONGER
//     determinism story than float sum (which is not associative-
//     stable) — see the parent TASK-0343.01 note.
//   - XOR shares the additive-identity ZERO with sum/or, so the
//     existing per-backend zero-init of `parity` is correct unchanged
//     (no identity-aware init — that is min/max/and, deferred to
//     TASK-0343.01.02).
//   - Partitioning over the OUTER input index `i` (NOT over the
//     accumulator bins `b`) makes each worker WHOLE-ARRAY-REPLICATE
//     `parity` and XOR-fold only its own i-band; the host XORs the 4
//     full partials element-wise. XOR of the disjoint per-band partials
//     equals the global parity (each input toggles its bin exactly
//     once; partition is a disjoint cover of the inputs). Mirrors
//     08-histogram/distributed EXACTLY (i-band partition, whole-array
//     replicate, host element-wise combine) — only the combine op
//     differs.
//
// What this example does NOT demonstrate
// --------------------------------------
//   - Non-zero-identity combine (min->MAX, max->MIN, and->all-ones):
//     those need identity-aware init and are deferred to
//     TASK-0343.01.02.
//   - Float reductions: PRD §10.1 demands bit-identity; integer XOR
//     only here.
//
// Numeric choice: i32 throughout, matching 08-histogram so the same
// committed `input.bin` (values pre-clipped to [0, BINS)) is reused.

const N    : usize = 256;     // total input length
const BINS : usize = 16;      // parity bin count

// Input array and the BINS-wide accumulator output. Each `data`
// symbol is assigned by exactly one dataflow statement (PRD §6.2.1).
data input  : i32[N];
data parity : i32[BINS];

// I/O kernels — effectful, ordering preserved, never duplicated.
kernel load_input  : ()          -> i32[N]    effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[BINS]) -> ()        effectful;

// Per-element bin-parity-toggle kernel — pure, reorderable. Returns
// `acc ^ 1` iff `value == bin`, else `acc`. The conditional /
// data-dependent indexing lives in the Rust kernel body (PRD §6.2.2:
// arbitrary Rust); the algorithm uses only the rectangular masked-
// accumulate shape 03/04/08 already prove bit-identical.
//
// `combine=xor` (TASK-0343.01.01): under partition=workers each worker
// whole-array-replicates `parity` and the host element-wise XORs the
// per-worker partials. XOR is associative+commutative ⇒ order-
// independent ⇒ bit-identical across all 7 tier-1 backends (PRD §10.1).
kernel bin_xor     : (i32, i32, i32) -> i32   pure combine=xor;

// Load the entire input on whoever owns I/O (the schedule decides).
input <-- load_input();

// Per-input rectangular masked accumulator. `parity` is pre-initialised
// to zero by the codegen (the XOR identity); each (i, b) iteration calls
// `bin_xor(parity[b], input[i], b)` which returns `parity[b] ^ 1` iff
// `input[i] == b`, else `parity[b]`. The fold builds the full per-bin
// parity across all N inputs and all BINS bins.
for i : 0 .. N {
    for b : 0 .. BINS {
        parity[b] <-- bin_xor(parity[b], input[i], b);
    }
}

save_output(parity);
